(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-classes-2027-077"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-classes-2027-077","title":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Mixed Subjects | Full Length Test 2","sourceTitle":"DVRUO Classes CS Test Series | Mixed Subjects | Full Length Test 2","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO CLASSES","providerKey":"classes","series":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","seriesId":"cs-classes-2027","num":77,"category":"other","year":"","format":"B","durationSec":10800,"info":{"orgName":"DVRUO Classes","batchName":"DVRUO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027","subjectName":"Mixed Subjects"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/GO CLASSES/GO Classes Test Series for GATE CSE 2027/016_exam_2459_GO Classes CS Test Series Mixed Subjects Full Length Test 2.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Which of the following sets of bit strings CANNOT be described with a regular expression?<br />","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>All bit strings whose number of zeros is a multiple of five</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>All bit strings starting with a zero and ending with a one</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>All bit strings with an even number of zeros</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>All bit strings with more ones than zeros</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394250\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following strings the DFA accepts?</p>\n\n<div style=\"text-align:center\">\n<figure class=\"image\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img alt=\"Figure $1:$ DFA recognizing binary strings with certain pattern\" src=\"media/98/987a344f1c955bdb26a0.png\" width=\"300\">\n<figcaption>Figure $1:$ DFA recognizing binary strings with certain pattern</figcaption>\n</figure>\n</div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Bitstrings that end in $1.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Bitstrings with an equal number of occurrences of $01$ and $10.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Bitstrings with at least one $1.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394248\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following strings match the regular expression $a^\\ast b b(a b \\mid b a)^\\ast$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$a b b$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$a a b a$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$a b b a$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$bbbaab$</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394247\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>It is known that the language $\\text{L} \\subseteq\\{a, b\\}^\\ast$ that consists of all strings that contain an equal number of $a$ 's and $b$ 's is context-free. Let $\\text{M}$ be the regular language $a \\ast b^\\ast .$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is (are) true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n\t<li>$\\text{L} \\cap \\text{M}$ is a context-free language.</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{L} \\cap \\text{M}$ is a regular language.</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{L} \\cap \\text{M}=\\left\\{a^n b^m \\mid n\\right.$ is a positive integer less than integer $\\left.m\\right\\}$.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>None</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>I only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>III only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I and III</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394245\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following regular expressions will not generate a string with two consecutive $1$s? (Note that $\\varepsilon$ denotes the empty string.)</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-roman\">\n\t<li>$(1+\\varepsilon)(01+0)^\\ast$</li>\n\t<li>$(01+10)^ \\ast$</li>\n\t<li>$(0+1) ^\\ast(0+\\varepsilon)$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>I only</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>II only</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>III only</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>I and II only</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394243\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following is the Language accepted by this automata?</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"118\" src=\"media/a4/a44acd57fee023598ff3.png\" width=\"403\"></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Set of all strings ending with $a.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Set of strings with at least one $a$ and an even number of $b\\text{&#x27;s}.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Set of strings with at least one $a$ and an even number of $b\\text{&#x27;s}$ following the last $a.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Set of strings ending with $a$ and an even number of $b\\text{&#x27;s}$ following the last $a.$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394241\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{L}_1=\\left\\{w w^R / w \\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\right\\} \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{L}_2=\\left\\{w c&nbsp;w^R / w \\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\right\\} \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{L}_3=\\left\\{w w / w \\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\right\\}<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich one of the following is TRUE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{L}_1$ is deterministic CFL</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{L}_2$ is deterministic CFL</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{L}_3$ is a CFL but not a deterministic CFL</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{L}_3$ is deterministic CFL</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394239\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":8,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Number of states in the minimal DFA that accepts the language described by the following regular expression is ________\n$$\n(a b+c d)^*(c \\varepsilon+\\emptyset b)\n$$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394237\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the tiny grammar $\\text{G}$ given below -<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\\text{E} &amp; \\rightarrow&nbsp;\\text{A} \\mid \\text{B} \\\\<br>\n\\text{A} &amp; \\rightarrow a \\mid c \\\\<br>\n\\text{B} &amp; \\rightarrow b \\mid c<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{G}$ is $ \\text{LL(1)}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{G}$ is $\\operatorname{SLR}(1)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{G}$ is $\\operatorname{LR}(1)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of the above</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394235\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Which of the following statements is/are true in context of the Two-Phase Locking Protocol?<br />","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>A shared lock allows a transaction to write a data item.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>More than one transaction can have a shared lock on an item at the same time.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>More than one transaction can have an exclusive lock on an item at the same time.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Deadlock can be avoided by releasing locks as early as possible.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394233\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p><br>\nWhich of the following statements is/are true in context of the Two-Phase Locking $(\\text{2PL})$ Protocol?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Conservative $\\text{2PL}$ is deadlock-free.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Rigorous $\\text{2PL}$ releases only write locks after commit, shared locks can be released before commit as well.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Lock upgrades are allowed during the shrinking phase of $\\text{2PL}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Strict $\\text{2PL}$ produces strict schedules.</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394231\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>How many of the following statements is/are false regarding Deadlock Handling in concurrency control protocols?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>A deadlock prevention protocol ensures deadlock never occurs.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>In Wound-Wait, an older transaction waits on a younger one.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>A wait-for graph has undirected edges between transactions.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>A wait-for graph with $5$ nodes but only $3$ in a cycle is not in a deadlock state.</p>"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394229\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>How many of the following statements are true regarding the Timestamp Protocol?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Deadlock is not possible with timestamp protocols.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>A transaction that arrives later to the system always has a smaller timestamp.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The precedence graph for the timestamp algorithm has edges from smaller timestamp transactions to larger ones.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>A write is only performed if transaction has a timestamp $\\geq$ the read timestamp for the data item.</p>"}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394227\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Please mark the functional dependencies that are consistent with the following table.</p>\n\n<p>$$\\begin{array}{|l|l|l|l|}<br>\n\\hline \\mathrm{A} &amp; \\mathrm{B} &amp; \\mathrm{C} &amp; \\mathrm{D} \\\\<br>\n\\hline 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 6 &amp; 2 \\\\<br>\n\\hline 2 &amp; 4 &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\\\<br>\n\\hline 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 5 &amp; 2 \\\\<br>\n\\hline 2 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 &amp; 2 \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}$$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{A} \\rightarrow \\text{D}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{AB} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CD}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{ABC} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}$</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394225\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following two transactions and schedule $\\text{S}$ (time goes from top to bottom).<br>\n$$\\begin{array}{c|c}<br>\n\\text { Transaction } \\text{T}_0 &amp; \\text { Transaction } \\text{T}_1 \\\\<br>\n\\hline r_0[\\text{A}] &amp; \\\\<br>\nw_0[\\text{A}] &amp; \\\\<br>\n&amp; r_1[\\text{A}] \\\\<br>\n&amp; r_1[\\text{B}] \\\\<br>\n&amp; c_1 \\\\<br>\nr_0[\\text{B}] &amp; \\\\<br>\nw_0[\\text{B}] &amp; \\\\<br>\nc_0 &amp;<br>\n\\end{array}$$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is conflict serializable(CS) But not view serializable(VS).</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is VS but not CS.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>S is both CS and VS</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{S}$ is neither CS, nor VS.</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"The order of a leaf node in a $\\text{B tree}$ is the maximum number of (value, data record pointer) pairs it can hold. Given that the block size is $1 \\mathrm{~K}$ bytes(i.e. 1024 bytes), data record pointer is $7$ bytes long, the value field is $9$ bytes long and a block pointer is $6$ bytes long, what is the order of the leaf node?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[46,46],"answerText":"46","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Not available</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394221\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume that a data file has an index consisting of $\\text{N}$ items, where $\\text{N}$ is large. If a binary search of the index is used to find an item, then, of the following, which best approximates the mean number of comparisons required to locate a specific index entry?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{(N}+1) / 2$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{N(N}-1) / 2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\left(\\log _2 \\text{N}\\right)-1$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{N} \\log _2 \\text{N}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394220\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\mathrm{R}(\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b})$ be a schema and $\\mathrm{Q} 1$ and $\\mathrm{Q} 2$ are queries on $\\mathrm{R}$.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-sql\" data-pbcklang=\"sql\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nQ1: SELECT * FROM R;\nQ2: (SELECT * FROM R) INTERSECT (SELECT * FROM R);</pre>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{Q1}$ and $\\text{Q2}$ produce the same answer.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The answer to $\\text{Q1}$ is always contained in the answer to $\\text{Q2}.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The answer to $\\text{Q2}$ is always contained in the answer to $\\text{Q1}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{Q1}$ and $\\text{Q2}$ produce different answers.</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394218\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Let $\\text{R}_1, \\text{R}_2, \\ldots,\\text{ }R_n$ be a decomposition of schema $\\text{U}$. Let $u(\\text{U})$ be a relation, and let $r_i=\\Pi_{\\text{R}_i}(u)$<br>\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$u \\subseteq r_1 \\bowtie r_2 \\bowtie r_3 \\bowtie r_4 \\bowtie \\ldots r_n$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$u \\supseteq r_1 \\bowtie r_2 \\bowtie r_3 \\bowtie r_4 \\bowtie \\ldots r_n$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$u=r_1 \\bowtie r_2 \\bowtie r_3 \\bowtie r_4 \\bowtie . . r_n$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None of the above</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394216\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>In relational algebra, the join operator $(\\bowtie)$ is logically redundant if we have additionally</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>intersection $(\\cap)$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>crossproduct $(\\times)$, select $(\\sigma)$, and project $(\\pi)$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>difference $(-)$ and union $(\\cup)$.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>crossproduct $(\\times)$ and difference $(-)$.</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394214\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A weak entity</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>inherits part of its key from the &quot;parent&quot; entities to which it is related.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>must have partial&nbsp;participation in its identifying relationship set.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>is an entity with no attributes besides its key.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>is never mapped to a table in conversion to a relational schema.</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394212\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Here's a relation $(\\mathrm{R})$, its attributes and its functional dependencies $(\\mathrm{F})$ :<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{R}(\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{D}, \\mathrm{E}) \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{F}=\\{\\mathrm{CD} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}, \\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}\\}<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is/are NOT in the functional dependency closure of $\\text{F}\\left(\\text{F}^{+}\\right)?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{AC} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{AE} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CD}$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394210\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume a link has a round-trip propagation delay of $100 \\mathrm{~msec}$. Also, assume an $8 \\mathrm{~Kbps}$ transmitter and a frame with $3$ bit sequence numbers. What is the minimum frame size that can guarantee a $50 \\%$ channel utilization assuming the go back $\\mathrm{N}$ protocol?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$100$ bits</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$800 / 7$ bits</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$800/13$ bits</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$200$ bits</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394208\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following IP addresses and their class, which pair is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$111.56.45.78 : \\textsf{Class B}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$221.34.7.82 : \\textsf{Class C}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$111.56.45.78 : \\textsf{Class A}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$221.34.7.82 : \\textsf{Class D}$</p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394206\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose a $100$ Mbps point-to-point link is being set up between Earth and a new lunar colony. The distance from the moon to Earth is $385,000 \\mathrm{~km}$ and data travels over the link at the speed of light $\\left(3 \\times&nbsp;10^&nbsp;8\\right.\\;\\mathrm{m} / \\mathrm{s})$.</p>\n\n<p>What is the bandwidth-delay product for the link?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$20$ MByte</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$16$ MByte</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$12$ MByte</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$24$ MByte</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394204\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following statements regarding TCP are false?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>A TCP transmitter normally interprets three duplicate ACKs to mean that, while data packets have been received out of order, all data is successfully being delivered.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>TCP is a Transport layer protocol.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Acknowledging every TCP packet with a separate ACK is useful because it keeps the transmitters&#x27; congestion window size constant.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>TCP is a network layer protocol.</p>"}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394202\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"The process of subnetting refers to what?<br />","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Stealing bits from the network portion of the address to create more sub networks</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Stealing bits from the subnet mask in order to create more sub networks</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Stealing bits from the host portion of the address to create more sub networks</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Stealing bits from the inverse mask in order to create more sub networks</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394200\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following protocol is used by the routers to send error messages when communicating with another IP address?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\textsf{IP}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\textsf{ICMP}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\textsf{MQTT}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\textsf{NTP}$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394198\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider datagram packet switching. $\\text{A}$ has $3$ packets to send to $\\mathrm{B}$, via $2$ switches. The transmission time of a packet is $10 \\mathrm{~ms}$ and the link propagation delay is $2 \\mathrm{~ms}$. All links are identical. Assuming no queuing or processing delay, if A starts transmitting at time $0,$ at what time is the data fully received by $\\text{B}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$36 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$48 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$56 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$66 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.3333},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394197\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":30,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Consider sending a $3000$ byte datagram (including $20$ bytes of IP header) into a link that has an MTU of $500$ bytes. How many fragments are generated?","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[7,7],"answerText":"7","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 7</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394195\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A router is blasting out IP packets whose total length (data plus header) is $1024$ bytes.</p>\n\n<p>Assuming that packets live for $10 \\mathrm{~sec}$, what is the maximum line speed the router can operate at without danger of cycling through the IP datagram identification number space?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$53.7 \\mathrm{~Mbps}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2.1 \\mathrm{~Mbps}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$5.37 \\mathrm{~Mbps}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$21 \\mathrm{~Mbps}$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394193\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose two hosts, $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B},$ are separated by $20,000$ kilometers and are connected by a direct link of $\\text{R}=2 \\mathrm{Mbps}$. Suppose the propagation speed over the link is $2.5 \\ast10^{&nbsp;8} \\mathrm{~meters} / \\mathrm{sec}$. Consider sending a file of $800,000$ bits from Host $\\text{A}$ to Host $\\text{B}.$ Suppose the file is sent continuously as one large message. What is the maximum number of bits that will be in the link at any given time?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$20971$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$167772$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$335544$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$335545$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394191\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>You want to transfer a file from Kanpur to Madras. For this problem, assume the following:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>The size of file is $122,000$ bytes. It will be transferred in $1,500$-byte data packets, of which $40$ bytes are taken up with headers. The size of acknowledgment packets is $40$ bytes including header. Every packet is acknowledged.</li>\n\t<li>The communication is bidirectional and the bandwidth is $12 \\mathrm{~Mbps}$ (megabits/sec) in each direction. Hosts can send and receive at the same time.</li>\n\t<li>The latency from Kanpur to Madras is $12 \\mathrm{~ms}$, as is the latency in the opposite direction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>With Stop-and-Wait, how long will it take to transfer the file? (Include the time to receive the acknowledgment for the final data packet.)</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$2052.2 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2108.8 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1022.8 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1091.02 \\mathrm{~ms}$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394189\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose $\\text{A}$ is connected to $\\text{B}$ via an intermediate router $\\text{R}$. The link $\\text{A – R}$ is instantaneous, but the $\\text{R}\\rightarrow \\text{B}$ link transmits only $14$ packet each second, one at a time. Assume $\\text{A}$ sends to $\\text{B}$ using sliding window protocol with window size $=4$. How large does the queue at $\\text{R}$ grow in steady state?</p>\n\n<p>Steady state is state of system after some time (say after $5$ minutes).</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"190\" src=\"media/5e/5e8294cd4a47b86e38a5.png\" width=\"408\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394187\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>&nbsp;Let $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ be two stations attempting to transmit on an Ethernet. Each has frames ready to send all the time; $\\text{A's}$ frames will be numbered $\\text{A1, A2}$, and so on, and $\\text{B's}$ similarly. Let $\\text{T}=51.2$ $\\mu$ be the exponential backoff base unit. Suppose $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ simultaneously attempt to send frame $1,$ collide, and happen to choose backoff times of $0 \\times \\text{T}$ and $1 \\times \\text{T}$, respectively, meaning $\\text{A}$ wins the race and transmits $\\text{A} 1$ while Bwaits. At the end of this transmission, $\\text{B}$ will attempt to retransmit $\\text{B} 1$ while $\\text{A}$ will attempt to transmit $\\text{A} 2$. These attempts will collide, but now $\\text{A}$ backs off for either $0 \\times \\mathrm{T}$ or $1 \\times \\mathrm{T}$, while $\\text{B}$ backs off for time equal to one of $0 \\times \\mathrm{T}, \\ldots, 3 \\times \\mathrm{T}$.</p>\n\n<p>Give the probability that $\\mathrm{A}$ wins this second backoff race after this collision; that is, $\\text{A's}$ choice of backoff time is less than $\\text{B's}.$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1/3$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$5 / 8$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1 / 2$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$3 / 8$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394185\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":36,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>A context-free grammar is said to be left recursive if it has a non-terminal A such that there is a derivation $\\mathrm{A} \\Rightarrow{ }^{+} \\mathrm{A} \\alpha$ for some sequence of grammar symbols $\\alpha$. Which of the following grammar is left-recursive?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Bb} ; \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{a} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cc} ; \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{b} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Aa} ; \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{c} \\\\\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Aa} \\mid \\mathrm{Bb} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Aa}|\\mathrm{Abc}| \\mathrm{c} \\mid \\mathrm{Sb} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{bb}\\\\\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Br} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cs} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{At}\\\\\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Aa}|\\mathrm{Bb}| \\mathrm{c} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ba} \\mid \\in \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{d}<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394183\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar.<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{ll}<br>\n\\text{S} &amp; ::=\\text{AB} \\\\<br>\n\\text{A} &amp; ::=\\text{a} \\\\<br>\n\\text{A} &amp; ::=\\text{BaB} \\\\<br>\n\\text{B} &amp; ::=\\mathrm{bbA}<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is FALSE?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The length of every string produced by the grammar is even.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>No string produced by the grammar has an odd number of consecutive $\\text{b&#x27;s}.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>No string produced by the grammar has three consecutive $\\text{a&#x27;s}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>No string produced by the grammar has four consecutive $\\text{b&#x27;s}.$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394181\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following statements :</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>We define middle $(\\mathrm{L})=\\left\\{x: \\exists y, z \\in \\Sigma^*,(yxz&nbsp;\\in \\mathrm{L})\\right\\}$. Then middle $\\left(\\left\\{a^n b^n c^n,n \\geq 0\\right\\}\\right)=a^*b^* c^*$.</li>\n\t<li>We define middle $\\text{(L}) =\\left\\{x: \\exists y, z \\in \\Sigma^*,(y x z \\in \\text{L})\\right\\}$. Then middle $\\left(\\left\\{a^n b^n c^n,n \\geq 0\\right\\}\\right)$ is regular.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the above statements are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $1$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Only $2$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394179\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>The Turing machine $\\left(\\left\\{q_0, q_1, q_2\\right\\},\\{0,1\\},\\{0,1, \\text{B}\\}, \\delta, q_0, \\text{B}\\right)$ has the following three transitions, and no others:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\delta\\left(q_0, 0\\right)=\\left(q_1, 1, \\text{R}\\right) \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\delta\\left(q_1, 1\\right)=\\left(q_2, 0, \\text{L}\\right) \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\delta\\left(q_2, 1\\right)=\\left(q_0, 1, \\text{R}\\right)<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The given TM, on input $0110,$ will produce output $1110$ when TM halts.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The given TM, on input $0110 ,$ will produce output $1010$ when TM&nbsp;halts.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The given TM, on input $0110,$ will run forever without halting.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The given TM halts on ALL strings.</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394177\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the paragraph $\\text{Y} :$</p>\n\n<p>Assume set $\\text{A}$ is a subset of set $\\text{B}.$ We know that if a property $\\text{P}$ is satisfied by ALL elements of $\\text{B}$ then it is also satisfied by ALL elements of $\\text{A}$ since $\\text{A}$ is a subset of $\\text{B}$.</p>\n\n<p>A student $\\mathrm{X}$ has the following argument about closure properties of DCFL set :</p>\n\n<p>We understand that the DCFL set(set of all DCFL languages) is not closed under Union. However, the DCFL set is a proper subset of the CFL set and the CFL set is closed under union. So how can a proper subset Not inherit the global properties of its parent set?</p>\n\n<p>So, by paragraph $\\text{Y},$ If the CFL set is closed under union then the DCFL set should also be closed under union since the DCFL set is a subset of the CFL set.</p>\n\n<p>Where is the mistake in the above argument by student $\\mathrm{X}?$&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>There is no mistake in student $\\text{X&#x27;s}$ argument and the DCFL set is closed under union.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The mistake is that the CFL set is not closed under union.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The mistake is that from the said argument we can only deduce that union of two DCFL is CFL. We can&#x27;t deduce that the union of two DCFL is DCFL.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The mistake is that the set of DCFL is equal to the set of CFL.</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394175\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following languages are Turing-recognizable?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\{\\langle \\text{M} \\rangle \\mid \\text{M}$ is a deterministic&nbsp;Turing machine and $\\text{M}$ accepts $010\\}$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\{\\langle \\text{M} \\rangle \\mid \\text{M}$ is a non-deterministic Turing machine and $\\text{M}$ accepts $010 \\}$.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\{\\langle \\text{M} \\rangle \\mid \\text{M}$ is a Turing machine and $\\text{M}$ does not accept $101 \\}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\left\\{\\langle \\text{M} \\rangle \\mid \\text{M} \\right.$ is a Turing machine and $\\left.\\text{L(M)}=\\Sigma^*\\right\\}$.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394174\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following languages :</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>The language of regular expression $(\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{1})^* \\mathbf{1 1}(\\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{1})^*$</li>\n\t<li>The language of regular expression $\\left(\\mathbf{0}^* \\mathbf{1}^* \\mathbf{1 1}\\right)^* \\mathbf{0}^* \\mathbf{1 1} \\mathbf{0}^* \\mathbf{1}^*$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$(1)$ is proper subset of&nbsp;$(2).$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$(2)$ is proper subset of&nbsp;$(1).$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$(1)=(2).$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Neither $(1)$ is subset of $(2),$ nor $(2)$ is subset of $(1).$</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394172\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Here is a context-free grammar $\\text{G}=(\\{\\text{S, A, B}\\},\\{0,1\\}, \\text{P, S})$, where $\\text{P}$ is the set of productions:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\text{S} \\rightarrow 0 \\text{A} \\mid 1 \\text{B} \\mid \\epsilon \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\text{A} \\rightarrow 1 \\text{S} \\mid 0 \\text{AA} \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\text{B} \\rightarrow 0 \\text{S} \\mid 1 \\text{BB}<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following statements is/are true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The languages generated by $\\mathrm{G}$ is set of strings which have equal number of $0\\text{&#x27;s}$ and $1\\text{&#x27;s}.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>If $\\mathrm{A}$ is the start symbol then the language generated by $\\mathrm{G}$ is set of strings with one more $1$ than $0.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>If $\\text{B}$ is the start symbol then the language generated by $\\mathrm{G}$ is set strings with one more $0$ than $1.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>String $1001, 101010$ are generated by $\\text{G}.$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394170\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"120\" src=\"media/07/07ede6b60214ea0fe227.png\" width=\"388\"></p>\n\n<p>In the figure above, a finite automaton $\\text{M}$ has start state $\\text{A}$ and accepting state $\\text{C}$. Which of the following regular expressions denotes the set of words accepted by $\\text{M}?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$10 ^\\ast 1 ^\\ast 0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$01(01)^\\ast$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$1(10) ^\\ast 0$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$1(01) ^\\ast 0$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394168\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following regular expressions is equivalent to (describes the same set of strings as) $\\left(a^\\ast +b\\right)^\\ast (c+d) ?$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$a^\\ast (c+d)+b(c+d)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$a^\\ast (c+d)^\\ast +b(c+d)^\\ast $</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$a^\\ast (c+d)+b^\\ast (c+d)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$(a+b)^\\ast c+(a+b)^\\ast d$&nbsp;</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394166\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following CFG, where the set of terminals is $\\{a, b, \\#, \\%, !\\}$ :<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\text{S} \\rightarrow \\% a \\text{T} \\mid \\text{U} ! \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\text{T} \\rightarrow a \\text{S} \\mid b a \\text{T} \\mid&nbsp;\\epsilon \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\text{U} \\rightarrow \\# a \\text{TU} \\mid \\epsilon<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The FIRST sets for each of the non-terminals&nbsp;$\\text{S, T, U}$ is as follows :<br>\n\t$$\\begin{aligned} \n\t\\& \\text{S}:\\{\\%, \\#, !\\} \\\\ \n\t\\& \\text{T}:\\{a, b, \\epsilon\\} \\\\ \n\t\\& \\text{U}:\\{\\#, \\epsilon\\} \n\t\\end{aligned}$$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The FOLLOW sets for each of the non-terminals&nbsp;$\\text{S, T, U}$ is as follows : $$\\begin{aligned} \\& \\text{S}:\\{\\#, !, \\$\\}\\\\ \\& \\text{T}:\\{\\#, !, \\$\\}\\\\ \\& \\text{U}:\\{!\\} \\end{aligned}$$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The grammar is $\\text{LL(1)}.$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The grammar is $\\text{LR(1)}.$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394164\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":47,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following left and right recursive grammars for the language $a ^\\ast$.</p><ul><li>$\\text{G1&nbsp;: S} \\rightarrow a \\text{S} \\mid \\epsilon$</li><li>$\\text{G2&nbsp;: S} \\rightarrow&nbsp;\\text{S} a \\mid \\epsilon$</li></ul><p>Which of the following is true about shift-reduce parsing of the above two grammars?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{G1}$ can be parsed with shift-reduce parser.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{G2}$ can be parsed with shift-reduce parser.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Shift-reduce parser for $\\text{G1}$ requires an unbounded stack size to parse large strings (like a million $a\\text{&#x27;s})$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Shift-reduce parser for $\\text{G2}$ requires a constant stack size to parse large strings (like a million $a\\text{&#x27;s})$</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394162\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar.<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{a} \\mathrm{S} \\text { b } \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{S} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{b}<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nWhich of the following statements is (are) true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>The grammar is ambiguous.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>The grammar is suitable for top-down parsing.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>The grammar is suitable for bottom-up parsing.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>The grammar is $\\operatorname{LR}(0)$</p>"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394160\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>This is a right-recursive grammar:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{aligned}<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{T} \\mid \\mathrm{T}+\\mathrm{E} \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{T} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{P} \\mid \\mathrm{P} \\ast \\mathrm{~T} \\\\<br>\n&amp; \\mathrm{P} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{id}<br>\n\\end{aligned}<br>\n$$<br>\nFollowing is the parse tree for sentence $x+y\\ast z$.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/79df97338366f4a5e45c.png\" width=\"300\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\nHow many handles are present in parse tree?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 8</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394158\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following grammar(s) have following two properties -</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:decimal\">\n\t<li>Enforces left-associativity of $+$ and $\\times$</li>\n\t<li>Enforces precedence of $\\times$ over $+$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned} \\&\\text{E} \\rightarrow \\text{id} \\mid \\text{E}+\\text{id} \\mid \\text{E} \\times \\text{id} \\mid(\\text{E}) \\\\ \\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned} \\&\\text{E} \\rightarrow \\text{id} \\mid i d+\\text{E} \\mid \\text{id} \\times \\text{E} \\mid(\\text{E}) \\\\ \\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}\\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{E} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{T}+\\mathrm{E} \\mid \\mathrm{T} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{T} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{P} \\times \\mathrm{T} \\mid \\mathrm{P} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\mathrm{P} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{id} \\mid(\\mathrm{E})\\\\\\\\<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\begin{aligned}<br>\n\t&amp; \\text{E} \\rightarrow \\text{E}+\\text{T} \\mid \\text{T} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\text{T} \\rightarrow \\text{T} \\times \\text{P} \\mid \\text{P} \\\\<br>\n\t&amp; \\text{P} \\rightarrow \\text{id} \\mid(\\text{E})<br>\n\t\\end{aligned}$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Here is a program in $\\textsf{IR}$ for computing the double factorial of $\\textsf{5 (5!!):}$</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/de567282beba596d03f1.png\" width=\"300\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;What will be the number of edges in the control flow graph?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$6$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$7$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$8$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$9$</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394154\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the following partial schedule for several data items for transactions $T_i$ with timestamp $i:$</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"268\" src=\"media/f6/f6e367a99a039f0dde69.png\" width=\"428\"></p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;How many transactions are Aborted by timestamp scheduler?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394152\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a concurrency control manager by timestamps. Below are several sequences of events, including start events, where $sti$ means that transaction $\\text{T}_i$ starts and coi means $\\text{T}_i$ commits. These sequences represent real time, and the timestamp-based scheduler will allocate timestamps to transactions in the order of their starts.<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\nWhich of the following schedules are allowed/generated by the timestamp scheduler i.e. No transaction is aborted by the timestamp scheduler?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$st1; st2; r1(A); r2(A); w1(B); w2(B)$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$st1; st2; r2(A); co2; r1(A); w1(A)$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$st1; st2; st3; r1(A); w3(A); co3 ; r2(B); w2(A)$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$st1; st2; st3; r1(A); w1(A); r2(A)$</p>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394150\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a relation $\\mathrm{R}(\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B})$ that contains $r$ tuples; assume $r&gt;0$. Make no assumptions about keys $\\&amp;$ assume that the domain of all attributes is integers.<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\nConsider the following queries:</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{Q1}:(\\text{R }\\bowtie&nbsp;\\text{R}) \\bowtie&nbsp;\\text{R}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{Q2}:\\sigma_{\\text{A&gt;B}} \\text{R} \\cup \\sigma_{\\text{A&lt;B}} \\text{R}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>For a relational algebra expression $\\mathrm{Q}$, Let $\\min (\\mathrm{Q}), \\max (\\mathrm{Q})$ be the minimum and maximum number of tuples that could be in the result of the expression $\\mathrm{Q}.$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is/are correct?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\min (\\mathrm{Q} 1)=0 ; \\min (\\mathrm{Q} 2)=0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\min (\\mathrm{Q} 1)=r ; \\min (\\mathrm{Q} 2)=0$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\max (\\mathrm{Q} 1)=rrr ; \\max (\\mathrm{Q} 2)=r$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\max (\\mathrm{Q} 1)=r ; \\max (\\mathrm{Q} 2)=r$</p>"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394148\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Here is a simple $\\text{B}^+$ tree with a root and $4$ leaves.</p><p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be69f12bd0032a43601c.png\" style=\"height:186px; width:543px\"></p><p>Assume that each node has room for $3$ keys and $4$ pointers, and the ordering of keys is alphabetic. Also assume that when a node is split, and there is an odd number of pointers, the new node with the larger number of pointers is to the left of the other. Suppose that we insert a record with key '$e$' into this tree. Draw the new tree. Which of the following would be the list of non-null keys in some node of the resulting tree (i.e. Key Content of some node)?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$k$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$e f g$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$f$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$dhk$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394146\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":56,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"Consider the following relational database tables $\\text{R , S}$ and $\\text{T}:$\n$$\\overset{\\text{R}}{\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\\hline \\hline \\mathrm{A} & \\mathrm{B} \\\\\n\\hline 1 & 2 \\\\\n3 & 2 \\\\\n5 & 6 \\\\\n7 & 8 \\\\\n9 & 8 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}} \\quad \n\\overset{\\text{S}}{\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\\hline \\hline \\text{B} & \\text{C} \\\\\n\\hline 6 & 2 \\\\\n2 & 4 \\\\\n8 & 1 \\\\\n8 & 3 \\\\\n2 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}} \\quad\n\\overset{\\text{T}}{\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\\hline \\hline \\mathrm{A} & \\mathrm{C} \\\\\n\\hline 7 & 1 \\\\\n1 & 2 \\\\\n9 & 3 \\\\\n5 & 4 \\\\\n3 & 5 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}}$$\nWhat is the number of tuples in the result of the following relational algebra query:\n$$\\text{R} \\bowtie \\text{S}  \\bowtie \\text{T}?$$","paragraph":null,"options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> 3</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394144\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider a database $\\textsf{COMPANY}.$ The following two tables in this database store the data of employees and their dependents.<br>\n&nbsp;<br>\n$\\textsf{Employee (Fname, Lname}, \\underline{\\textsf{Ssn}}, \\textsf{Sex, Dno)}$<br>\n$\\textsf{Dependent} (\\underline{\\textsf{Essn, Dependent_name}}, \\textsf{Sex, Relationship)}$</p>\n\n<p>Now consider the following two SQL queries.<br>\n$\\textsf{Q1:}$</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-sql\" data-pbcklang=\"sql\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nSELECT E.Fname, E.Lname\nFROM EMPLOYEE AS E\nWHERE E.Ssn IN ( SELECT Essn\n            FROM DEPENDENT AS D\n            WHERE E.Fname=D.Dependent-name AND E.Sex=D.Sex );\n</pre>\n\n<p><br>\n$\\textsf{Q2 :}$</p>\n\n<pre class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-sql\" data-pbcklang=\"sql\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\">\nSELECT E.Fname, E.Lname\nFROM EMPLOYEE AS E, DEPENDENT AS D\nWHERE E.Ssn=D.Essn AND E.Sex=D.Sex AND E.Fname=D.Dependent-name;\n</pre>\n\n<p><br>\nWe want to Retrieve the name of each employee who has a dependent with the same first name and is the same sex as the employee.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\textsf{Q1}$ produces the desired result, $\\textsf{Q2}$ does not.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\textsf{Q2}$ produces the desired result, $\\textsf{Q1}$&nbsp;does not.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both $\\textsf{Q1, Q2}$ produce the desired result.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Neither $\\textsf{Q1}$ nor $\\textsf{Q2}$ produces the desired result.</p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394142\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Which of the following $\\text{ER}$ diagram represents the given requirements</p>\n\n<p>$\\text{“Every professor teaches exactly one course”}$</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/db/db78eb132281ae8e0a98.png\" width=\"300\">"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a5c5cfa2b29c7ba23ffe.png\" width=\"300\">"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/76/7638ca2760e58743344c.png\" width=\"300\">"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e5/e501231a98554cbd2062.png\" width=\"300\">"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>During the negotiation period, the transmitter and receiver have agreed to use the polynomial $1001$ for their CRC code. Suppose that a receiver has received the following frame $11001001010.$</p>\n\n<p>Consider the following statements :</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$\\text{S1}:$ The receiver Should reject the frame.</li>\n\t<li>$\\text{S2}:$ receiver will get the remainder of all zeroes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Only $\\text{S1}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Only $\\text{S2}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Both</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>None</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394138\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Assume a link has a round-trip propagation delay of $100 \\mathrm{~msec}$. Also, assume an $8\\;\\text{Kbps}$ transmitter and a frame with $3$ bit sequence numbers. What is the minimum frame size that can guarantee a $50 \\%$ channel utilization assuming the stop-and-wait protocol?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$50$ Bytes</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$100$ Bytes</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$150$ Bytes</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$200$ Bytes</p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394136\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>For the network shown below, use Dijkstra's algorithm to find the optimal route from $\\text{A}$ to $\\text{J}.$</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"237\" src=\"media/e6/e63950ff61b555a209a9.png\" width=\"520\"></p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\text{A C  H E J}$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\text{A C  E  J H}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\text{A C  J  H E}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\text{A C  E  H J}$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394134\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Suppose computers $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ have $\\textsf{IP}$ addresses $10.105.1.113$ and $10.105.1.91$ respectively and they both use the same netmask $\\mathrm{N}$. Which of the values of $\\mathrm{N}$ given below should not be used if $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ should belong to the same network?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .0$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .128$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .192$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$255.255 .255 .224$</p>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394132\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Below table is a routing table using $\\textsf{CIDR}.$ Address bytes are in hexadecimal. The notation $\\textsf{“/12\"}$ in $\\textsf{C4.50.0.0/12}$ denotes a netmask with $12$ leading $1$ bits, that is, $\\textsf{FF.F0.0.0}.$<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{l|l}<br>\n\\hline \\text{Net/MaskLength} &amp; \\text{NextHop} \\\\<br>\n\\hline \\textsf{C4.5E.2.0/23} &amp; \\textsf{A} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{C4.5E.4.0/22} &amp; \\textsf{B} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{C4.5E.C0.0/19} &amp; \\textsf{C} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{C4.5E.40.0/18} &amp; \\textsf{D} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{C4.4C.0.0/14} &amp; \\textsf{E} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{C0.0.0.0/2} &amp; \\textsf{F} \\\\<br>\n\\textsf{80.0.0.0/1} &amp; \\textsf{G} \\\\<br>\n\\hline<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nState to what next hop the following will be delivered.</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$\\textsf{C4.5E.03.87}$ will be delivered to $\\textsf{B}$.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$\\textsf{C4.5E.7F.12}$ will be delivered to $\\textsf{D}$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$\\textsf{C4.5E.D1.02}$ will be delivered to $\\textsf{C}$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$\\textsf{C4.5E.D1.02}$ will be delivered to $\\textsf{B}$</p>"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> B, C</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394130\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the hosts $\\text{W, X, Y, Z}$ and the learning bridges $\\text{B1, B2, B3}.$ Assume, that they have empty forwarding tables to begin.<br>\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9ba40c243f4ed069a384.png\" width=\"300\"><br>\nSuppose the following two events happens in the order.</p>\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>Host $\\text{X}$ sends a packet to $\\text{W}$</li>\n\t<li>Host $\\text{Z}$ sends a packet to $\\text{Y}.$</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>Now, finally, host $\\text{W}$ sends to $\\text{Y}.$ Which of the following is/are true for this last transmission?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>Bridge $\\text{B2}$ will learn of the location of $\\text{W}.$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>Bridge $\\text{B3}$ will learn of the location of $\\text{W}.$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>Host $\\text{Z}$ will see this packet.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>Host $\\text{X}$ will see this packet.</p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A, B, C, D</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394128\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"","html":"<p>Consider the original grammar, now with numbered productions:<br>\n$$<br>\n\\begin{array}{l|l}<br>\n1 &amp; \\text{S} \\rightarrow&nbsp;\\text{CC} \\\\<br>\n2 &amp; \\text{C} \\rightarrow&nbsp;\\text{Cc} \\\\<br>\n3 &amp; \\text{C} \\rightarrow&nbsp;\\text{d}<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\n$$<br>\nHere is its $\\text{LR(1)}$ parse table:<br>\n\\begin{array}{l|lll|l|}&nbsp;<br>\n&amp; c &amp; d &amp; \\$ &amp; \\text{C} \\\\<br>\n\\hline 0 &amp; &amp; s 4 &amp; &amp; 1 \\\\<br>\n1 &amp; s 3 &amp; s 4 &amp; &amp; 2 \\\\<br>\n2 &amp; s 3 &amp; &amp; \\mathrm{acc} &amp; \\\\<br>\n3 &amp; r 2 &amp; r 2 &amp; r 2 &amp; \\\\<br>\n4 &amp; r 3 &amp; r 3 &amp; r 3 &amp;<br>\n\\end{array}<br>\nSuppose we want to parse string $\\text{“dcd\"}$ then what will be state of $\\text{LR}$ parser just before consuming last symbol?</p>","paragraph":null,"options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>$1$</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>$2$</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>$3$</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>$4$</p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.6667},"solution":"<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A</p><p><a href=\"https://gateoverflow.in/394126\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Official discussion page</a></p>"}],"sectionsMeta":[{"name":"Technical","marks":100.0,"instructions":null}],"math":true,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":65}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":36,"MSQ":22,"NAT":7},"withoutKey":0}};
